From d1fd67bb607b8d988c79763bd5799c9c3fbea473 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cruz <cruz@tbu.net>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 10:04:44 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 046/206] feat(shorebird): ResolveDDSlots and
 --print_dd_resolution_to

Tenth of their eleven private flags. Only print_shorebird_info is left, and it
genuinely needs the three-stage mapper.

This was recorded as blocked on the DD IL subsystem. It is not, and the reason
it looked blocked was the "DD" in its name -- the same mistake that had been
made about dd_function_identity. The slots come from the DDSlotVoters file, not
from IndirectStaticCallInstr or a DD table, so the algorithm is drivable and
checkable today.

ResolveDDSlots builds a FunctionKey -> owner index by walking libraries ->
classes -> functions plus one synthetic ForAllocStub key per class, then votes
per slot. A vote counts only when both the caller and target keys resolve to
exactly one owner; the winner is the TARGET. Unresolved slots fall back to the
previous resolution. Called once per DoCompileAll from two points, so the second
call is verify-only and preserves the first call's decisions.

Verified against their own gen_snapshot on the pipeline's fixture:

  - pass 2's report line is BYTE-IDENTICAL to theirs (0 resolved, 7 preserved,
    7 carried, 1 dropped, empty_tally=1 [tgt_miss=1], from 2983 functions)
  - pass 1 matches on every resolution counter; only the key map size differs
    (2896 vs 2878), a call-site timing difference of 18 functions
  - all 8 rows of the resolution TSV are identical, including the sentinel at
    slot 2 and the exact function name in every resolved slot

Negative controls: returning the caller instead of the target resolves every
slot to the voter's caller function (Map._fromKeyValues, _objectHashCode, ...)
and turns both the TSV comparison and the new unit test red (255). That swap
leaves every structural property intact -- the stream parses, slots resolve,
the counters still read "7 resolved" -- so nothing weaker than comparing the
winning function's identity would catch it.

KNOWN GAPS, deliberately not papered over:
  - the TSV's `rewritten` column and the header's rewritten/ counts come from
    the DD table rewrite step, which does need the DD IL subsystem. We emit 0
    rather than guess, so our header reads rewritten=0 where theirs reads 2.
  - plurality tie-breaking is unexercised: every fixture we can build has one
    voter per slot. Theirs falls back to libc++ hash order, ours to
    first-encountered. A passing oracle run does not confirm that branch.
  - `refreshed` and `dropped-stale` are always 0; no fixture reaches them.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>

